   "start_page": 207,
   "summary": "This subfile forms the concluding portion (pages 13 through 32 of Report No. 1676, plus appended lists) of an earlier duplicate filing of Despatch No. 5682. It reviews Japanese military operations in Shansi, Hupeh, Chekiang, and Kwangtung, along with Ambassador Kumatarō Honda's consultations in Tokyo urging the political and economic reinforcement of the Nanking regime. It details the May 6 economic convention and agreement signed with French Indochina, the Franco-Thai peace treaty, the expansion of Japanese demands in Batavia, ratification of the Soviet-Japanese Neutrality Treaty, the establishment of the nationalistic Tōhōkai party by Seigō Nakano, and new commodity price and trade controls. The document concludes with complete chronological schedules of telegrams (Nos. 620-755) and despatches (Nos. 5556-5630) sent by Embassy Tokyo to the Department of State during May 1941.",
   "title": "Report of Conditions in Japan During the Month of May 1941 (Report No. 1676; Despatch No. 5682, Duplicate Copy)",
   "to": "Cordell Hull, Secretary of State, Washington"
  },
  {
   "about": "Official despatch transmitting Report No. 1676 on political, diplomatic, and economic conditions in Japan during May 1941, including Ambassador Grew's interviews with Foreign Minister Matsuoka.",
