   "start_page": 238,
   "summary": "Ambassador Joseph C. Grew transmits Report No. 1676 reviewing Japanese political, diplomatic, and economic conditions during May 1941. The report opens with detailed accounts of Grew's May 14 and May 19 conversations with Foreign Minister Yōsuke Matsuoka concerning American naval convoys to Britain, the interpretation of Article 3 of the Tripartite Alliance, and Japan's peaceful intentions versus threats regarding the southward advance. It proceeds through Embassy representations over Japanese bombings of American properties in China, Japan's refusal to retract abrogation of the Fur Seal Convention, relations with China and European powers, domestic political movements, and tight economic regulations. Appended to the report are the opening pages of the chronological register of telegrams dispatched to Washington during the month.",
   "title": "Despatch No. 5682: Report of Conditions in Japan During the Month of May 1941 (Report No. 1676)",
   "to": "Cordell Hull, Secretary of State, Washington"
  }
